<section class="section section--tight">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="answer"><strong>The short answer:</strong> a GST return is mainly data, not paperwork. You enter or upload invoice details and summaries, and you generally do not upload the bills. But you must still keep the invoices and records behind those figures, because they are what you rely on if your return is ever questioned.</p>

      <h2 id="two">Two different lists</h2>
      <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Filing data and records kept, scrolls sideways on small screens">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">To prepare the return, you need</th><th scope="col">Behind it, you must be able to show</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Sales invoice data, including customer GSTINs for business sales</td><td>The tax invoices themselves</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Credit and debit note details</td><td>The notes, and why they were issued</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Purchase details and your GSTR-2B</td><td>Supplier invoices, and proof of receipt of goods or services</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tax payable and available credit</td><td>Your credit register and payment records</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Portal login and your approval to file</td><td>Filed returns and acknowledgements</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <h2 id="monthly">A preparation list for each return period</h2>
      <h3>For sales (GSTR-1)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Sales register, or billing software export</li>
        <li>Invoices, including any cancelled ones, for the sequence to make sense</li>
        <li>Credit and debit notes</li>
        <li>Advance receipts, if relevant</li>
        <li>Marketplace or POS reports if you sell through them</li>
        <li>E-invoice or e-way bill data, if these apply to you</li>
        <li>HSN or SAC codes for what you sell</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>For the summary and credit (GSTR-3B)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Purchase register and supplier invoices</li>
        <li>The current GSTR-2B</li>
        <li>Your action on invoices in the Invoice Management System</li>
        <li>Details of reverse charge purchases, if any</li>
        <li>Items where credit is blocked or should be reversed</li>
        <li>Your electronic cash and credit ledger balance</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="law">What the law says about records</h2>
      <p>Section numbers below were read in CBIC's copy of the Act as amended to 1 January 2022, so check for later changes. Rule 56 comes from secondary explanations.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Where and what:</strong> section 35 requires a true and correct account, at the principal place of business on your registration, of matters such as supplies, stock, input tax credit, and tax payable and paid.</li>
        <li><strong>What:</strong> rule 56 adds detail, such as records of advances and of goods or services imported, exported or under reverse charge.</li>
        <li><strong>How long:</strong> section 36 requires 72 months from the due date of the annual return for the year. If you are party to an appeal or proceeding, or under investigation, the records concerned must be kept for one year after it ends, or for the 72 months, whichever is later.</li>
        <li><strong>In what form:</strong> rule 56 allows records in electronic form, subject to conditions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="mistakes">Mistakes that cost people money</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Relying on the portal as your record.</strong> It shows what was reported, not necessarily what happened. Keep your own invoices.</li>
        <li><strong>Claiming credit from memory.</strong> Claims should tie to invoices you hold and to your GSTR-2B position.</li>
        <li><strong>Losing thermal-paper bills.</strong> They fade, so scan them early.</li>
        <li><strong>Keeping everything on the accountant's laptop.</strong> Records are your responsibility, so keep your own copy.</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring nil periods.</strong> If you are registered, you may still need to file with nothing to report.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="need">Do you need a professional?</h2>
      <p>Many small businesses can prepare this data themselves and use the {% ext "https://www.gst.gov.in/", "GST portal" %} directly. If your purchases do not match your GSTR-2B, or you are behind, it may be worth asking for help. See {% routeLink "/services/gst-return-filing/", "what to expect from GST return help" %}.</p>
      <p>For wider monthly paperwork, use the {% routeLink "/guides/monthly-accounting-documents-checklist/", "monthly documents checklist" %}.</p>

